        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section">
        <div class="container container-narrow">
            <article class="prose">
                <p>QR codes have become an essential bridge between the physical and digital worlds. From restaurant menus to contactless payments and marketing campaigns, these matrix barcodes provide a seamless experience for users. However, not all QR codes are created equal. This guide explains the differences between code types and how you can create professional, branded QR codes for free without worrying about expiration dates.</p>
                
                <h2>Static vs. Dynamic QR Codes</h2>
                <p>The most important choice when generating a code is deciding between a static or dynamic format. Most "free" online generators give you a dynamic code, which means the code points to their server first, and they redirect it to your URL. The danger here is that they can charge you later or disable the link whenever they want.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Static QR Codes:</strong> The data (like your website URL) is encoded directly into the pattern of the QR code itself. These codes never expire because they don't depend on a middle-man server. FormFix generates pure static codes.</li>
                    <li><strong>Dynamic QR Codes:</strong> These codes allow you to change the destination URL later, but they require a subscription and a central server to manage the redirect.</li>
                </ul>
                
                <h2>The Importance of Branding</h2>
                <p>A standard black-and-white QR code can look generic and unappealing. Branded codes include your company logo or custom colors, which can increase scan rates by up to 30%. When users see a familiar brand icon inside the code, their level of trust increases, making them more likely to interact with your content.</p>

                <h2>Technical Best Practices for Scanning</h2>
                <p>To ensure your QR code scans reliably every time, across all smartphone models:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>High Contrast:</strong> Always keep the dots significantly darker than the background. Light-colored dots on a dark background can fail on older cameras.</li>
                    <li><strong>Quiet Zone:</strong> Maintain a clear white border around the outside of the code. If text or graphics touch the code, the scanner may fail to recognize the pattern.</li>
                    <li><strong>Avoid Over-Complexity:</strong> The more data you put in a static code (like a very long URL), the denser the pattern becomes. Use a URL shortener if your link is exceptionally long.</li>
                </ul>

                <h2>How to Generate your Code on FormFix</h2>
                <p>Creating your custom code is easy and requires no technical expertise:</p>
                <ol>
                    <li>Navigate to the <a href="/qr-code-generator">QR Code Generator</a>.</li>
                    <li>Choose your content type: URL, WiFi Network, VCard (Business Card), or Plain Text.</li>
                    <li>Input your details. You will see the QR code update in real-time.</li>
                    <li>Customize appearance: Pick a primary color that matches your brand and upload a logo icon to place in the center if desired.</li>
                    <li>Download as a high-resolution PNG for printing or digital use.</li>
                </ol>

                <h2>Why Use FormFix for QR Generation?</h2>
                <p>Most generators are designed for "lead magnets"—they want your email or a monthly fee. FormFix is a utility. Our generator is 100% private, runs entirely in your browser, and produces high-quality, permanent codes that belong to you forever. No redirects, no expirations, and no hidden costs.</p>

